}
const softwareRenderer = detectSoftwareRenderer()
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({canvas, antialias: !softwareRenderer,...})
```
Creating an extra context could be okay but may log. Alternatively use document `navigator.userAgent` no. Probe is fine, but may consume WebGL contexts and trigger warnings. Test environment one page. Use `powerPreference:'high-performance'`, a context might choose hardware if available. In SwiftShader, detection.

Maybe simply create renderer with antialias true then if software set; cannot change antialias. This is fine.

Actually renderer internally has `_antialias`, but changing not recommended. Could recreate canvas? no.

Let's not over-engineer. DPR 0.65 and shadows reduced. Also freeze shadow updates after initial:
```
if (softwareRenderer) {
 renderer.shadowMap.autoUpdate = false
